#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aecfac4d-b8c4-5591-b623-0fa8c192ac80" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "322f1808-370e-599c-854f-a59f96fec337" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={42}^{∘},{138}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278101} {:answer/id #uuid "64b89554-fb75-51cc-b307-823fa7b33e83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={28}^{∘},{132}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278102} {:answer/id #uuid "0eb6a9c9-dcb6-59d4-b6da-dc83c86cfed6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={32}^{∘},{128}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278103} {:answer/id #uuid "81b425dd-bd8d-5863-aaa6-2aefe9adee1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={52}^{∘},{138}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278104} {:answer/id #uuid "9b1fa623-b950-5f80-8cbd-9ba9ed3d620f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={42}^{∘},{158}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "322f1808-370e-599c-854f-a59f96fec337" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={42}^{∘},{138}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278101} :answer-field/id #uuid "b06000d4-cd35-503a-910d-7b21a3172aae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cb50220b-8323-5baa-a0d8-fb501062acd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-120828" :question/problem "Calculate, in degrees, rounded to the nearest integer, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\frac{2}{3}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{2}{3})=41.810{…}^{∘}≈{41.81}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${41.81}^{∘}$ lies inside the range, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={41.81}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={41.81}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the second quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={41.81}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/assets/bc0c031fe6ccbb40dc77474582b301456f537ee2a649ba2d5b3bca9b1240618f.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{41.81}^{∘} \\\\  & ={138.19}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions (rounded to the nearest integer) are $x={42}^{∘},{138}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6fa751e-c9a4-5f02-b858-d841f0e6ce70" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Quadrantal Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "293e029f-767f-54c3-a403-dcd4fc60238d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278108} {:answer/id #uuid "cdebb2e3-f88b-5c18-ba79-e5686a1fd339" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278109} {:answer/id #uuid "231ec4a1-9496-51e1-80f1-49e777b3d092" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={270}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278110} {:answer/id #uuid "50be34b5-4fce-54c9-a3ce-1d702298b875" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278111} {:answer/id #uuid "9abf62cf-85c4-52ed-a6e8-daa30ed11128" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdebb2e3-f88b-5c18-ba79-e5686a1fd339" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278109} :answer-field/id #uuid "e451ba94-f8ea-5321-ab3f-a13fc9a3fc14" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "49d94093-5a76-5a48-9933-3ad26a6829ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-120866" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 1 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1)={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${90}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$ \n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its maximum values at:\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n\n$$\n\nSince the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x={90}^{∘}$ is the only solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278113 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aecfac4d-b8c4-5591-b623-0fa8c192ac80" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b95adefa-7f9b-5fbd-9348-57914717722c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278115} {:answer/id #uuid "483206c2-f753-5306-a63d-6718303e5bd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "224.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278116}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "483206c2-f753-5306-a63d-6718303e5bd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "224.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278116} :answer-field/id #uuid "d51ba88d-3984-5504-b2bb-1fb572037baa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278114} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91ddd2ef-0b80-558d-83db-c280a121d555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "315.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278118}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91ddd2ef-0b80-558d-83db-c280a121d555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "315.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278118} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb7b8933-68f8-597e-90ad-b6bda4d254a2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "abb3e962-4f1e-5cbf-bc32-5a4e2bf1bf6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-332216" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation  $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-0.7,$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$   are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$, $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-0.7)=-44.427\\,004{…}^{∘}≈-{44.4270}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $x=-{44.4270}^{∘}$ is not in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ To get the first solution that's within the required domain, we need to find an angle that's coterminal with $-{44.4270}^{∘}.$ To do this, we simply add ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-{44.4270}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={315.5730}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={44.4270}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={44.4270}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/assets/f3d2d41e1a58dd562ed47ae7e5fcdc79382c426700f520a1153111bfcf2d734b.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{44.4270}^{∘} \\\\  & ={224.4270}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈{224.4}^{∘},\\,x≈{315.6}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "28f4d12d-55f3-5551-a993-70590cc67cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine Under a Modified Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da6df80f-cb26-504f-a511-d13fe4c4ec4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-143.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278121}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da6df80f-cb26-504f-a511-d13fe4c4ec4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-143.1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278121} :answer-field/id #uuid "91c566bf-1b9a-5182-b920-b27871eb5ed8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278120} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96b7e0d0-47a6-5b36-a24b-feb79a90a247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96b7e0d0-47a6-5b36-a24b-feb79a90a247" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278123} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7421400-eb46-5433-af97-68e8b0a57320" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0cf4768e-f324-53aa-93ec-e41e3ef947d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-336283" :question/problem "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-0.6,$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘},$ are\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$, $\\,x≈$\n{{field-2}}${}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-0.6)=-36.869\\,897…≈-36.8699\n$$\n\nSince $-{36.8699}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-{36.8699}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={36.8699}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={36.8699}^{∘}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13977985/assets/8d88b4ccfe08c26c9d56effaf651e04703995ffaec4b7ab08187f62ddd1897de.png)\n\nA second solution $X$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, and we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{36.8699}^{∘} \\\\  & ={216.8699}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, this value is not in the required domain. To get the second solution ${x}_{2}$ that's within the given domain, we subtract ${360}^{∘}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}=X-{360}^{∘} & ={216.8699}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} \\\\  & =-{143.1301}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to one decimal place, the solutions are \n\n$$\nx≈-{143.1}^{∘},\\,x≈-{36.9}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6fa751e-c9a4-5f02-b858-d841f0e6ce70" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation Involving Sine with Quadrantal Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6885791f-17a0-5a8a-8946-d191751e68c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278126} {:answer/id #uuid "a6181791-d0ab-58a6-99a7-960792133ebe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={120}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278127} {:answer/id #uuid "075ed122-8179-5c8e-91c0-f846c8f8b869" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278128} {:answer/id #uuid "60b3784c-b2e0-5340-9600-404d28bb21b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278129} {:answer/id #uuid "70af95ab-7259-59a4-85b5-660396077ff0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6885791f-17a0-5a8a-8946-d191751e68c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x={90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278126} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f3fa86d-79a9-58ec-a821-71100c8e8c2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9bcb1e6a-aa69-5e79-931d-b04daae57f14" :question/math-academy-id "q-72782" :question/problem "Solve the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2=0$ for $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1)={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${90}^{∘}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$ \n\nAs this is a quadrantal angle, to find the remaining solutions we use our knowledge of the sine function. We know that the sine function has its maximum values at:\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}\\cdot n\n$$\n\nSince the domain $-{180}^{∘}<x\\le {180}^{∘}$ is one period, and we have already found one maximum value in this period, we conclude that $x={90}^{∘}$ is the only solution."}]}
